            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"In biblical symbolism, plural 'branches' primarily represent believers in living union with Christ (John 15) or corporate members of God's covenant people, such as Israel and Gentiles, who can be grafted in or broken off (Romans 11).\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Branches symbolize the members of a nation who can be broken off or grafted in while the tree remains standing.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Believers united to Christ, or members of the covenant community (Israel/Gentiles).\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Members of a nation.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book restricts the meaning of branches entirely to the corporate or national metaphor of Romans 11, omitting the highly prominent personal discipleship imagery of John 15 (believers connected to Christ the vine).\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None, as the book accurately captures the Romans 11 usage recognized by the consensus, but it does not account for the John 15 usage.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The consensus recognizes both the John 15 (individual believers) and Romans 11 (covenant people/Israel) metaphors as standard meanings. The book elevates the Romans 11 imagery ('members of a nation', 'grafted in') as the sole definition, refining the scope to a subset of the accepted consensus.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Excluding the John 15 imagery of disciples abiding in Christ the Vine leaves out the most prominent New Testament use of the symbol.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
